# Hypothesis — when did summed social capital peak, and which components rise?

**Answer.** Summed social capital peaks in the **13th century BCE (1300–1201 BCE; 3250–3151 calBP; CE signed −1300 to −1201)**, with an exact tie against the preceding
**14th century BCE bin (1400–1301 BCE; 3350–3251 calBP; CE signed −1400 to −1301)**. Under 150/200/250-year smoothing the 13th-century bin wins outright (3 of 5 windows);
under 50/100-year windows the two bins tie exactly. Robust statement: **a social-capital maximum spanning 1400–1201 BCE, best single century = 13th c. BCE.**

Social capital = Copper & Gold + Amber + Monument Count (simple sum of raw Z values, per the study framework).

**Which individual components increase in that window (pre-registered rule: ≥2 of {≥25% of the peak anomaly, Δ vs series mean ≥ +0.5 Z, positive 500-year slope}):**
1. **Copper & Gold** (component "Copper & Gold", column ZCU_AU) — Z = +2.54 (95th percentile of its own series), +2.54 above its series mean, **59.9% of the whole peak anomaly**, positive trend over the preceding 500 years. Meets 3/3 criteria.
2. **Monument Count** (column ZMonument) — Z = +1.71 (97th percentile), +1.71 above its mean, **40.3% of the peak anomaly**, positive 500-year trend. Meets 3/3 criteria.
3. **Amber** (column Zamber) — **does NOT increase**: Z = −0.01 at the peak (67th percentile), −0.01 relative to its mean, −0.3% of the anomaly. Its own maximum lies much earlier, in the 4th millennium BCE (3300–3000 BCE, Z = +1.54). It fails 2 of 3 criteria.

**Hypothesis in natural language.** Summed social capital in the Central-European Neolithic–Bronze Age sequence reaches its maximum in the Late Bronze Age, in the
13th century BCE (1300–1201 BCE ≈ 3250–3151 calBP; the adjacent 1400–1301 BCE bin is tied with it, so the safest formulation is "the 14th–13th centuries BCE, peaking
in the 13th"). The peak is not a general upswing of all capital forms: it is driven almost entirely by two of the three social-capital components — **Copper & Gold
(~60% of the peak anomaly) and Monument Count (~40%)** — which rise together and together account for essentially 100% of it (the third component's contribution is
statistically zero and the additive decomposition confirms the three contributions sum exactly to the anomaly, +4.238 Z-sum units). This verdict is identical in both
tied bins (1400–1301 and 1300–1201 BCE), so the component answer does not depend on which side of the tie is chosen. **Amber does not participate**: amber had already
declined from its 4th-millennium BCE peak and sits at its long-run mean when the social aggregate peaks. The peak is social-specific **in the 13th-century bin**: there the cultural-capital block (pottery form and
decoration diversity) is below its mean (28th/39) and so is the economic block (Depot, Sickle, Axes & Celts, Monument Size; 34th/39, Z-score −0.52), while only the
symbolic block (Hatchets & Swords, Daggers, House Size) is simultaneously high (2nd/39, Z-score +2.19) although its own maximum lies two centuries later
(1800–1701 BCE). In the tied 14th-century bin the picture is less exclusive — the economic block is also elevated there (3rd/39, +2.43), driven by Depot and
Monument Size — so the "social only" reading is strongest for 1300–1201 BCE. Mechanistically we therefore read the peak as a **metallurgical (copper/gold) + monumental-construction phenomenon — prestige goods
plus collective labour mobilisation — rather than as an across-the-board prosperity or exchange-network maximum**; amber, the long-distance exchange proxy, is
decoupled from it. Landscape-openness (pollen) support is weak and reported as secondary: SC vs pollen PC1 proxies gives r = 0.02–0.32 (best r = 0.32 for Belau PC1
lagged 2 centuries, n = 36 overlapping bins), so we do **not** invoke demography/landscape opening as the mechanism.

**Caveats.** (i) "Summed social capital" is an ad-hoc index: the sum of Z values standardised within each 39-bin series, so it is comparable across centuries but not
in absolute magnitude and not comparable to other blocks in absolute units. (ii) The two-bin tie is not numerical noise — the underlying capital.csv rows for 1400 and
1300 BCE are identical (forward-filled values), so it is a data plateau. (iii) Conceptual overlap inside the framework: Monument Count is assigned to *social*
capital while Monument Size is assigned to *economic* capital — same material domain, count vs scale; we confirm this allocation is the study's, and it means the
monumental signal is split across two blocks. (iv) The pollen record ends at 702 BCE, so no openness evidence exists for the last two centuries of the cultural series.
(v) ZSichel (sd 0.54, 74% zeros) is not standardised on the same footing as the others; it only affects the economic comparison block.
